  title="What Exterior Painting Costs in Middle Tennessee, and What Moves the Number"
  description="What exterior house painting costs in Middle Tennessee, why quotes vary so widely, the rot repair trap, and how to compare painting bids line by line."
>
<p>Across the Middle Tennessee market, a full exterior repaint on a typical house most often gets quoted somewhere in the low four figures to the low five figures. That is a wide range, and the width is the honest answer, because two houses on the same street can be thousands of dollars apart once size, siding material, stories, and wood condition come into play. This guide explains what actually moves an exterior painting number, the hidden item that blows up more painting budgets than anything else, and how to compare bids so you know what you are buying. These are market observations, not a price list. Every house earns its own number, which is why we quote flat, per project, after looking at yours.</p><h2>The five things that move the number most</h2><h3>1. How much surface is actually getting painted</h3><p>This sounds obvious and is still the biggest source of confusion. A brick home where only the trim, soffits, fascia, shutters, and doors get painted is a fundamentally smaller job than a house with painted siding on every wall. Plenty of Middle Tennessee brick ranches fall into that first bucket and their owners overestimate the cost, while owners of fully sided two-story homes often underestimate theirs. Before you collect quotes, walk the house and be clear about what is paint and what is brick, because trim-only and full-body repaints are different projects with different budgets.</p><h3>2. Stories and access</h3><p>Height costs money. Second-story work means ladders, staging, and slower production, and steep lots or tight side yards slow it down further. A story and a half of walkout basement on the back of a Franklin hillside home can add real labor to what looked like a one-story job from the street.</p><h3>3. Siding material and condition</h3><p>Smooth fiber cement takes paint efficiently. Older wood siding and trim need more prep, more primer, and more repair. Peeling, chalking, and previous coats that were applied over dirt or moisture all mean more preparation hours, and preparation is where most of a quality exterior job's labor lives. A repaint over sound, previously well-maintained surfaces is a much smaller job than a rescue of a house that has not been painted in fifteen years.</p><h3>4. The rot you cannot see yet</h3><p>Here is the item that blows up more exterior painting budgets than any other: wood repair. Middle Tennessee humidity, clogged gutters, and sprinklers work on fascia boards, window sills, brick mold, and the bottoms of trim runs year after year. Paint fails there first, water gets behind it, and by repaint time some of that wood is soft. Paint over rot and the new paint fails in a season, so any painter doing the job right will replace the bad wood first, and any quote that does not mention wood repair at all is either assuming your house is perfect or planning to surprise you. Ask every bidder how discovered rot gets handled and priced. It is the difference between a number that holds and a number that grows.</p><h3>5. Product and coats</h3><p>Paint lines vary a lot in durability, and one coat versus two is a real difference in both cost and lifespan. A cheaper bid often quietly means one coat of a builder-grade product, which is how a repaint comes due again in four years instead of eight or ten. The product name and coat count belong in writing on every quote.</p><h2>When to paint in Middle Tennessee, and how long it lasts</h2><p>Timing is a quality issue here, not just a scheduling one. Exterior paint wants moderate temperatures, dry surfaces, and time to cure, which makes spring and fall the natural windows in Middle Tennessee. Summer work is done all the time, but July heat flashes paint dry too fast on sun-hit walls, and our humidity slows curing everywhere else. Winter work depends on the product, since most exterior paints have minimum application temperatures that our January does not reliably respect. A good crew works around all of this by chasing shade and watching dew points, which is one more thing prep-focused painters do that the lowest bid usually does not.</p><p>As for lifespan, expectations should be surface by surface. Body paint on fiber cement holds up the longest, wood trim and anything facing south or west weathers years sooner, and horizontal surfaces like handrails and porch floors fail first everywhere. That is why the smart cycle is not repaint everything every so many years, it is a full repaint on the long cycle with trim touch-ups and recaulking in between, catching the south face and the sills before they fail open.</p><h2>Rentals are their own math</h2><p>For landlords and property managers, full exterior repaints are periodic capital work, but the exterior items that actually protect the asset run smaller and more often: touching up trim and doors between tenants, repainting a weathered front door, recaulking joints, and catching failing fascia paint before the water gets in. Those small exterior lines fold naturally into turnover work and regular maintenance visits, which is the same logic covered in our guide to <a href='/articles/handyman-cost-middle-tennessee/'>handyman work in Middle Tennessee</a>. The expensive version of exterior painting is the deferred version. Five years of skipped touch-ups converts into wood repair plus a full repaint, on the schedule the rot picks rather than the one you pick.</p><h2>If you are painting to sell</h2><p>Exterior paint is consistently one of the higher-return projects before listing, because it is most of what a buyer sees in the first photo and the first walk-up. That does not always mean a full repaint. A power wash, a painted front door, fresh shutters, and clean trim lines deliver a large share of the curb appeal for a fraction of the cost. We ranked exterior paint against the other usual candidates in <a href='/articles/pre-listing-repairs-worth-doing/'>which repairs are worth doing before you list</a>.</p><h2>How to compare painting quotes</h2><p>Quotes that are thousands of dollars apart are usually describing different jobs. Put every bid through the same questions:</p><ul><li><strong>Prep, in writing.</strong> Wash, scrape, sand, prime bare wood, caulk gaps. The words that are missing from the cheap quote are usually these.</li><li><strong>Wood repair.</strong> How is rot found, priced, and approved? Is any repair included, or is it all extra?</li><li><strong>Product and coats.</strong> Named paint line, named sheen, and the number of coats, per surface.</li><li><strong>What is included.</strong> Doors, shutters, gutters and downspouts, foundation vents, railings, and the back side of the house all either are or are not in the number.</li><li><strong>Protection and cleanup.</strong> Landscaping, windows, concrete, and daily cleanup.</li><li><strong>Insurance and warranty.</strong> Proof of insurance, and what the workmanship warranty covers and for how long.</li></ul><p>A flat, itemized quote against that checklist is a number you can trust for budgeting. An hourly guess or a one-line total is not, and the gap between the two is where most painting disappointment lives.</p><h2>Getting a real number for your house</h2><p>Market ranges are for orientation. The only number that matters is the one for your walls, your trim, and your wood condition. Upkeep runs exterior painting across Middle Tennessee with the rest of our trades behind it, which matters on exactly this kind of job, because the crew that finds soft fascia can also replace it instead of painting around it. Every job is quoted flat before work begins, so the number you approve is the number you pay. Tell us about your project through our <a href='/work-order/'>service request form</a> or see how we run <a href='/services/painting/'>painting work</a>, and we will put a firm number on it.</p>
